1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,207
It's eleven at night.

2
00:00:01,660 --> 00:00:03,547
Marco is alone in the bakery.

3
00:00:03,773 --> 00:00:04,716
And on the bench?

4
00:00:04,905 --> 00:00:06,641
A jar of sourdough starter.

5
00:00:07,094 --> 00:00:08,679
He fed it at nine...

6
00:00:08,716 --> 00:00:10,867
when it stood just four centimeters tall.

7
00:00:11,320 --> 00:00:13,132
His rubber band marks eight,

8
00:00:13,207 --> 00:00:14,007
double that.

9
00:00:14,188 --> 00:00:15,396
When it reaches the band,

10
00:00:15,433 --> 00:00:17,094
the morning's dough can be mixed.

11
00:00:17,358 --> 00:00:19,245
His notes say this starter doubles about

12
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:20,679
every four hours.

13
00:00:21,056 --> 00:00:22,150
It's been two.

14
00:00:22,415 --> 00:00:24,301
So how tall should it be right now?

15
00:00:24,679 --> 00:00:25,584
Halfway to the band?

16
00:00:26,037 --> 00:00:26,943
Not quite.

17
00:00:27,169 --> 00:00:28,754
By the end, you'll work out that height

18
00:00:28,867 --> 00:00:29,667
yourself.

19
00:00:29,962 --> 00:00:32,754
On the way: what a function really is,

20
00:00:32,792 --> 00:00:34,415
how to read one from a graph,

21
00:00:34,490 --> 00:00:36,603
and the exponent rules calculus leans on

22
00:00:36,754 --> 00:00:37,660
all year.

23
00:00:37,962 --> 00:00:38,762
Why start here?

24
00:00:39,169 --> 00:00:41,735
Every calculus question hands you a function,

25
00:00:41,811 --> 00:00:44,000
as a formula, a graph, a table,

26
00:00:44,075 --> 00:00:44,875
or a sentence.

27
00:00:45,283 --> 00:00:47,471
And plenty of exam points vanish before

28
00:00:47,547 --> 00:00:50,415
any calculus starts: a missing parenthesis,

29
00:00:50,490 --> 00:00:52,528
or a negative exponent moved the wrong way.

30
00:00:52,981 --> 00:00:54,566
So we fix those now.

31
00:00:58,066 --> 00:01:00,330
A function is a rule that takes an input

32
00:01:00,481 --> 00:01:03,047
and gives back exactly one output.

33
00:01:03,273 --> 00:01:05,537
We write f of x for the output when the

34
00:01:05,613 --> 00:01:06,896
input is x.

35
00:01:07,122 --> 00:01:10,216
So f of x equals x squared minus three x

36
00:01:10,367 --> 00:01:13,311
means: square the input, then subtract three

37
00:01:13,386 --> 00:01:14,556
times the input.

38
00:01:14,820 --> 00:01:16,556
The x is just a placeholder,

39
00:01:16,632 --> 00:01:18,518
an empty slot waiting for a number.

40
00:01:18,971 --> 00:01:21,839
To evaluate, put the input into every slot,

41
00:01:21,915 --> 00:01:23,311
and put it in parentheses.

42
00:01:23,575 --> 00:01:25,990
f of negative two is negative two,

43
00:01:26,066 --> 00:01:29,009
in parentheses, squared, minus three times

44
00:01:29,084 --> 00:01:30,066
negative two.

45
00:01:30,141 --> 00:01:32,481
That's four plus six, so ten.

46
00:01:32,933 --> 00:01:35,198
Without the parentheses, the square grabs

47
00:01:35,273 --> 00:01:37,613
only the two, and you get negative four.

48
00:01:37,990 --> 00:01:39,877
One missing pair of parentheses,

49
00:01:39,915 --> 00:01:41,688
and the answer is wrong.

50
00:01:41,764 --> 00:01:43,500
The input can be a whole expression,

51
00:01:43,613 --> 00:01:44,234
too.

52
00:01:44,254 --> 00:01:46,367
What's f of a plus one?

53
00:01:46,443 --> 00:01:48,556
Every x becomes a plus one,

54
00:01:48,632 --> 00:01:49,575
in parentheses.

55
00:01:49,764 --> 00:01:52,405
Expand and collect, and you get a squared

56
00:01:52,556 --> 00:01:54,518
minus a minus two.

57
00:01:54,594 --> 00:01:56,216
Notice that's not f of a,

58
00:01:56,254 --> 00:01:57,235
plus one.

59
00:01:57,311 --> 00:01:59,273
The function acts on the whole input at

60
00:01:59,349 --> 00:02:00,179
once.

61
00:02:00,254 --> 00:02:02,669
Some inputs, a rule can't take.

62
00:02:02,745 --> 00:02:03,726
Which ones?

63
00:02:03,801 --> 00:02:06,443
The domain is the set of inputs it accepts,

64
00:02:06,481 --> 00:02:08,933
and the range is the set of outputs it actually

65
00:02:09,009 --> 00:02:09,809
makes.

66
00:02:09,839 --> 00:02:12,254
For now, two things shrink a domain.

67
00:02:12,330 --> 00:02:14,367
Logarithms will add a third later.

68
00:02:14,443 --> 00:02:16,858
A denominator can never be zero.

69
00:02:16,896 --> 00:02:19,650
And a square root needs zero or more.

70
00:02:19,801 --> 00:02:21,990
Try h of x equals a fraction.

71
00:02:22,066 --> 00:02:24,858
On top, the square root of x plus four.

72
00:02:24,933 --> 00:02:26,971
On the bottom, x minus one.

73
00:02:27,047 --> 00:02:29,462
The root needs x plus four to be at least

74
00:02:29,537 --> 00:02:32,669
zero, so x is at least negative four.

75
00:02:32,707 --> 00:02:34,330
The bottom can't be zero,

76
00:02:34,367 --> 00:02:36,216
so x can't be one.

77
00:02:36,254 --> 00:02:38,971
Domain: x at least negative four,

78
00:02:39,047 --> 00:02:40,971
and x not equal to one.

79
00:02:41,009 --> 00:02:43,122
Negative four itself is fine: the square

80
00:02:43,198 --> 00:02:45,198
root of zero is zero.

81
00:02:45,235 --> 00:02:47,235
Marco's jar is a function too.

82
00:02:47,273 --> 00:02:48,971
Call it s, for starter.

83
00:02:49,009 --> 00:02:51,172
The input is the time since he fed it,

84
00:02:51,223 --> 00:02:52,153
in hours.

85
00:02:52,204 --> 00:02:53,801
The output is the height.

86
00:02:53,839 --> 00:02:56,632
At any one moment, the jar has exactly one

87
00:02:56,971 --> 00:02:57,366
height.

88
00:02:57,386 --> 00:02:58,417
And the domain?

89
00:02:58,468 --> 00:03:00,594
It starts at zero, the feeding.

90
00:03:00,632 --> 00:03:03,084
Before nine, this batch didn't exist yet,

91
00:03:03,122 --> 00:03:05,990
so the situation itself limits the domain.

92
00:03:06,066 --> 00:03:09,462
So s of zero is four, and s of two is what

93
00:03:09,499 --> 00:03:11,084
he's waiting to find.

94
00:03:11,122 --> 00:03:12,971
Your turn, two quick ones.

95
00:03:13,009 --> 00:03:16,330
Let g of x equal x squared plus two x.

96
00:03:16,518 --> 00:03:18,254
Find g of negative three.

97
00:03:18,292 --> 00:03:20,455
Then, the domain of this fraction.

98
00:03:20,506 --> 00:03:23,311
On top, the square root of x minus two...

99
00:03:23,349 --> 00:03:25,613
and on the bottom, x minus five.

100
00:03:32,088 --> 00:03:33,522
Let's check the first one.

101
00:03:33,598 --> 00:03:35,484
Negative three, in parentheses,

102
00:03:35,635 --> 00:03:37,333
squared, gives you nine.

103
00:03:37,371 --> 00:03:39,296
Then two times negative three?

104
00:03:39,333 --> 00:03:40,767
That's minus six.

105
00:03:40,843 --> 00:03:43,522
So g of negative three is three.

106
00:03:43,560 --> 00:03:46,466
The trap is squaring without the parentheses.

107
00:03:46,503 --> 00:03:48,805
That gives negative nine minus six,

108
00:03:48,843 --> 00:03:50,239
negative fifteen.

109
00:03:50,277 --> 00:03:52,994
For the domain: the root needs x minus two

110
00:03:53,069 --> 00:03:56,352
at least zero, so x is at least two.

111
00:03:56,390 --> 00:03:58,881
The bottom needs x not equal to five.

112
00:03:58,956 --> 00:04:02,805
So x at least two, and x not five.

113
00:04:06,305 --> 00:04:09,475
The graph of f is every point x comma f

114
00:04:09,550 --> 00:04:10,381
of x.

115
00:04:10,494 --> 00:04:12,796
So f of a is a height.

116
00:04:12,871 --> 00:04:14,909
Go to a on the horizontal axis,

117
00:04:14,984 --> 00:04:17,223
then go up or down to the graph,

118
00:04:17,274 --> 00:04:19,135
and read how high it is there.

119
00:04:19,211 --> 00:04:21,626
Graphs in calculus often come in pieces,

120
00:04:21,777 --> 00:04:23,286
so watch the dots.

121
00:04:23,399 --> 00:04:26,003
A filled dot is a point on the graph.

122
00:04:26,079 --> 00:04:28,192
An open circle marks a point the graph comes

123
00:04:28,343 --> 00:04:30,305
close to, but doesn't include.

124
00:04:30,381 --> 00:04:33,249
Here, at x equals one, the open circle at

125
00:04:33,362 --> 00:04:36,242
height three point five is not on the graph.

126
00:04:36,293 --> 00:04:38,758
The filled dot at height five is.

127
00:04:38,909 --> 00:04:41,022
So f of one is five.

128
00:04:41,060 --> 00:04:43,034
A few more things a graph tells you.

129
00:04:43,085 --> 00:04:45,211
To solve f of x equals k,

130
00:04:45,249 --> 00:04:47,487
draw the flat line y equals k,

131
00:04:47,538 --> 00:04:49,249
and read the x values where it meets the

132
00:04:49,664 --> 00:04:50,134
graph.

133
00:04:50,154 --> 00:04:52,418
f is positive where the graph is above the

134
00:04:52,569 --> 00:04:53,651
x axis.

135
00:04:53,751 --> 00:04:55,739
And the range runs from the lowest height

136
00:04:55,815 --> 00:04:57,324
to the highest.

137
00:04:57,399 --> 00:04:59,286
Marco keeps a chart of his starter taped

138
00:04:59,324 --> 00:05:01,324
to the wall, against the clock.

139
00:05:01,399 --> 00:05:02,343
Here's tonight.

140
00:05:02,381 --> 00:05:04,192
Before the feeding, the old starter had

141
00:05:04,267 --> 00:05:06,041
risen to nine centimeters.

142
00:05:06,079 --> 00:05:08,494
At nine, he poured most of it out and topped

143
00:05:08,532 --> 00:05:10,506
it up with fresh flour and water.

144
00:05:10,557 --> 00:05:13,060
The height dropped to four in an instant.

145
00:05:13,098 --> 00:05:15,211
On the chart, that's an open circle at the

146
00:05:15,286 --> 00:05:17,098
old height, and a filled dot at the new

147
00:05:17,173 --> 00:05:20,468
one: at nine o'clock, the jar is at four.

148
00:05:20,519 --> 00:05:22,381
A rule that switches formulas like that

149
00:05:22,456 --> 00:05:24,041
is a piecewise function.

150
00:05:24,116 --> 00:05:26,758
Here's one: x squared when x is less than

151
00:05:26,833 --> 00:05:29,852
two, and three x minus one when x is two

152
00:05:29,928 --> 00:05:30,871
or more.

153
00:05:30,909 --> 00:05:32,418
So how do you use it?

154
00:05:32,494 --> 00:05:34,909
First, ask which piece the input belongs

155
00:05:35,173 --> 00:05:35,493
to.

156
00:05:35,513 --> 00:05:37,777
then use only that formula.

157
00:05:37,852 --> 00:05:39,022
And the boundary point?

158
00:05:39,060 --> 00:05:41,324
It belongs to the piece that says or equal

159
00:05:41,626 --> 00:05:42,247
to.

160
00:05:42,267 --> 00:05:45,148
So f of two uses three x minus one.

161
00:05:45,199 --> 00:05:46,116
That's five.

162
00:05:46,154 --> 00:05:47,777
And f of one point nine?

163
00:05:47,815 --> 00:05:50,456
That one uses x squared: three point six

164
00:05:50,532 --> 00:05:51,266
one.

165
00:05:51,286 --> 00:05:52,796
Boundaries like this are where calculus

166
00:05:52,909 --> 00:05:54,129
gets interesting...

167
00:05:54,255 --> 00:05:55,852
so go slowly there.

168
00:05:55,890 --> 00:05:56,758
Your turn!

169
00:05:56,796 --> 00:05:59,966
Here's f of x: x plus one when x is under

170
00:06:00,041 --> 00:06:03,173
three, and two x when x is three or more.

171
00:06:03,211 --> 00:06:04,833
What's f of three...

172
00:06:04,871 --> 00:06:07,022
and what's f of two point nine?

173
00:06:07,060 --> 00:06:09,701
And the tricky one: is the point three comma

174
00:06:09,777 --> 00:06:11,060
four on the graph?

175
00:06:17,535 --> 00:06:19,762
Three goes with the or equal to sign,

176
00:06:19,799 --> 00:06:22,403
so f of three is two times three,

177
00:06:22,441 --> 00:06:23,246
six.

178
00:06:23,297 --> 00:06:25,271
Two point nine is less than three,

179
00:06:25,309 --> 00:06:28,516
so it uses x plus one: three point nine.

180
00:06:28,554 --> 00:06:29,950
And three comma four?

181
00:06:29,988 --> 00:06:31,950
That's where the first piece is heading,

182
00:06:32,026 --> 00:06:33,724
but it stops just short.

183
00:06:33,762 --> 00:06:36,051
It's an open circle, so no,

184
00:06:36,076 --> 00:06:37,621
it's not on the graph.

185
00:06:37,686 --> 00:06:40,743
The trap is plugging three into x plus one,

186
00:06:40,781 --> 00:06:42,969
just because it's the formula written first.

187
00:06:46,469 --> 00:06:49,111
You'll draw a lot of lines in calculus.

188
00:06:49,186 --> 00:06:52,356
The slope of a line, m, is rise over run:

189
00:06:52,432 --> 00:06:56,054
y two minus y one, over x two minus x one.

190
00:06:56,092 --> 00:06:58,167
The little ones and twos just label two

191
00:06:58,243 --> 00:06:59,043
points.

192
00:06:59,111 --> 00:07:00,658
Subtract in the same order,

193
00:07:00,809 --> 00:07:01,866
top and bottom.

194
00:07:02,016 --> 00:07:04,281
Through one comma four and three comma negative

195
00:07:04,394 --> 00:07:06,771
two, that's negative six over two.

196
00:07:06,847 --> 00:07:08,583
A slope of negative three,

197
00:07:08,658 --> 00:07:10,620
so the line goes down to the right.

198
00:07:10,733 --> 00:07:13,413
Calculus writes lines in point slope form.

199
00:07:13,488 --> 00:07:16,281
y minus y one equals m, times the quantity

200
00:07:16,507 --> 00:07:17,941
x minus x one.

201
00:07:18,092 --> 00:07:20,658
Here, y minus four equals negative three,

202
00:07:20,771 --> 00:07:22,847
times the quantity x minus one.

203
00:07:22,922 --> 00:07:23,790
Why this form?

204
00:07:23,979 --> 00:07:26,205
Calculus usually knows one point and the

205
00:07:26,243 --> 00:07:27,149
slope there.

206
00:07:27,224 --> 00:07:29,035
And the exam accepts it as is.

207
00:07:29,488 --> 00:07:31,677
A composite function feeds one function's

208
00:07:31,752 --> 00:07:32,960
output into another.

209
00:07:33,337 --> 00:07:36,205
f of g of x means: do g first,

210
00:07:36,281 --> 00:07:37,903
then do f to the result.

211
00:07:38,092 --> 00:07:39,790
Work from the inside out.

212
00:07:39,979 --> 00:07:42,847
With f of x equals x squared and g of x

213
00:07:42,998 --> 00:07:44,658
equals three x minus one,

214
00:07:44,733 --> 00:07:47,450
f of g of x is three x minus one,

215
00:07:47,526 --> 00:07:48,507
all squared.

216
00:07:48,583 --> 00:07:51,375
But g of f of x is three x squared,

217
00:07:51,450 --> 00:07:52,432
minus one.

218
00:07:52,583 --> 00:07:54,092
Not the same at all.

219
00:07:54,167 --> 00:07:55,601
Order matters.

220
00:07:55,677 --> 00:07:57,262
Now, moving graphs.

221
00:07:57,413 --> 00:08:00,356
f of x, plus k, adds k to every height,

222
00:08:00,432 --> 00:08:02,394
so the graph moves up k.

223
00:08:02,545 --> 00:08:04,884
Negative f of x turns every height upside

224
00:08:05,149 --> 00:08:07,639
down, so it flips over the x axis.

225
00:08:07,677 --> 00:08:10,998
And f of x minus h moves it right h.

226
00:08:11,073 --> 00:08:13,035
That one feels backward, doesn't it?

227
00:08:13,111 --> 00:08:14,922
Marco's chart shows why.

228
00:08:15,035 --> 00:08:17,035
Say he had fed the starter at eleven instead

229
00:08:17,111 --> 00:08:19,564
of nine, two hours later on the clock.

230
00:08:19,601 --> 00:08:22,016
It grows the same way, so it reaches every

231
00:08:22,205 --> 00:08:23,639
height two hours later.

232
00:08:23,677 --> 00:08:25,866
The whole curve on his chart slides right

233
00:08:26,016 --> 00:08:26,884
by two.

234
00:08:26,922 --> 00:08:29,224
To read the new jar at clock time t,

235
00:08:29,262 --> 00:08:31,413
read the old curve two hours earlier,

236
00:08:31,450 --> 00:08:32,922
at t minus two.

237
00:08:32,960 --> 00:08:35,450
A minus sign, and a move to the right.

238
00:08:35,876 --> 00:08:36,834
Okay, your turn.

239
00:08:36,872 --> 00:08:39,023
Let f of x equal x squared,

240
00:08:39,061 --> 00:08:41,551
and g of x equal x minus four.

241
00:08:41,740 --> 00:08:45,136
Find f of g of two, and g of f of two.

242
00:08:45,174 --> 00:08:47,639
Then: how does the graph of x plus three,

243
00:08:47,690 --> 00:08:50,382
all squared, sit compared with x squared?

244
00:08:56,857 --> 00:08:58,065
Inside first.

245
00:08:58,140 --> 00:08:59,951
g of two is negative two,

246
00:09:00,027 --> 00:09:02,027
and f of negative two is four.

247
00:09:02,065 --> 00:09:03,348
Now the other way around.

248
00:09:03,423 --> 00:09:06,517
f of two is four, and g of four...

249
00:09:06,593 --> 00:09:07,574
is zero.

250
00:09:07,687 --> 00:09:08,782
Four versus zero!

251
00:09:09,159 --> 00:09:10,442
Order matters.

252
00:09:10,668 --> 00:09:13,989
And x plus three is x minus negative three,

253
00:09:14,065 --> 00:09:15,838
so the graph moves left three.

254
00:09:16,102 --> 00:09:18,291
The trap is reading the plus sign as a move

255
00:09:18,329 --> 00:09:19,272
to the right.

256
00:09:22,772 --> 00:09:25,565
Calculus has one short rule for every power

257
00:09:25,640 --> 00:09:28,508
of x, but it only sees powers.

258
00:09:28,583 --> 00:09:31,074
So first, you rewrite fractions and roots

259
00:09:31,149 --> 00:09:32,433
as exponents.

260
00:09:32,508 --> 00:09:35,149
An exponent counts repeated multiplication:

261
00:09:35,300 --> 00:09:38,206
x cubed is x times x times x.

262
00:09:38,470 --> 00:09:40,810
Marco's starter shows exactly that.

263
00:09:40,923 --> 00:09:43,074
His rough rule: every four hours,

264
00:09:43,187 --> 00:09:44,131
it doubles.

265
00:09:44,282 --> 00:09:46,470
After one stretch, twice as tall.

266
00:09:46,508 --> 00:09:47,980
And after three stretches?

267
00:09:48,093 --> 00:09:51,225
two times two times two: two cubed,

268
00:09:51,300 --> 00:09:52,734
eight times as tall.

269
00:09:52,810 --> 00:09:55,074
The exponent counts the doublings.

270
00:09:55,149 --> 00:09:57,414
Real starters start slowly and level off

271
00:09:57,489 --> 00:09:59,527
near their peak, but steady doubling is

272
00:09:59,565 --> 00:10:00,961
a good first model.

273
00:10:01,036 --> 00:10:03,602
Every rule comes from counting factors.

274
00:10:03,678 --> 00:10:05,791
x cubed times x squared.

275
00:10:05,866 --> 00:10:06,999
How many factors?

276
00:10:07,074 --> 00:10:08,961
Three, then two more.

277
00:10:09,036 --> 00:10:09,836
Five.

278
00:10:09,866 --> 00:10:11,678
So you add the exponents.

279
00:10:11,753 --> 00:10:14,546
x to the fifth over x squared: two factors

280
00:10:14,772 --> 00:10:16,357
cancel, three are left.

281
00:10:16,433 --> 00:10:17,716
So you subtract.

282
00:10:17,866 --> 00:10:21,263
x cubed, all squared, is two groups of three.

283
00:10:21,376 --> 00:10:23,602
Six factors, so you multiply.

284
00:10:23,678 --> 00:10:26,369
And x cubed over x cubed is one,

285
00:10:26,420 --> 00:10:29,187
but the subtraction rule says x to the zero.

286
00:10:29,338 --> 00:10:32,621
So any nonzero number to the zero is one.

287
00:10:32,659 --> 00:10:35,225
Now, why should x to the negative two mean

288
00:10:35,300 --> 00:10:37,036
one over x squared?

289
00:10:37,187 --> 00:10:39,602
Take x cubed over x to the fifth.

290
00:10:39,678 --> 00:10:42,218
Canceling leaves one over x squared.

291
00:10:42,269 --> 00:10:44,433
The subtraction rule says x to the power

292
00:10:44,508 --> 00:10:45,904
three minus five.

293
00:10:45,942 --> 00:10:47,942
That's x to the negative two.

294
00:10:47,980 --> 00:10:50,357
Both are right, so they're the same thing.

295
00:10:50,395 --> 00:10:52,659
And a negative exponent never makes a number

296
00:10:52,734 --> 00:10:53,565
negative.

297
00:10:53,640 --> 00:10:56,093
Two to the negative three is one eighth,

298
00:10:56,131 --> 00:10:58,043
a small positive number.

299
00:10:58,067 --> 00:10:59,980
The most expensive mistake here?

300
00:11:00,168 --> 00:11:01,263
Moving too much.

301
00:11:01,338 --> 00:11:03,527
An exponent applies only to the base it

302
00:11:03,602 --> 00:11:04,508
touches.

303
00:11:04,583 --> 00:11:07,149
Three times x to the negative two is three

304
00:11:07,225 --> 00:11:08,546
over x squared.

305
00:11:08,583 --> 00:11:10,282
Only the x goes down.

306
00:11:10,319 --> 00:11:12,458
But three x, in parentheses,

307
00:11:12,483 --> 00:11:16,093
to the negative two is one over nine x squared.

308
00:11:16,131 --> 00:11:18,923
So five over x cubed, minus one over two

309
00:11:19,149 --> 00:11:21,992
x, becomes five x to the negative three,

310
00:11:22,118 --> 00:11:24,659
minus one half x to the negative one.

311
00:11:24,734 --> 00:11:26,395
The two is just a number,

312
00:11:26,470 --> 00:11:28,546
so it stays as one half.

313
00:11:28,583 --> 00:11:30,508
Now fractional exponents.

314
00:11:30,546 --> 00:11:32,772
What should x to the one half be?

315
00:11:32,810 --> 00:11:35,149
Square it, and you double the exponent.

316
00:11:35,225 --> 00:11:37,225
One half times two is one,

317
00:11:37,263 --> 00:11:39,149
so you get plain x.

318
00:11:39,225 --> 00:11:41,414
So x to the one half is the number whose

319
00:11:41,489 --> 00:11:44,483
square is x: the square root of x,

320
00:11:44,533 --> 00:11:46,055
for x at least zero.

321
00:11:46,093 --> 00:11:48,244
In a fraction exponent, the bottom is a

322
00:11:48,282 --> 00:11:50,470
root, and the top is a power.

323
00:11:50,546 --> 00:11:52,772
Root first keeps the numbers small.

324
00:11:52,810 --> 00:11:55,074
Eight to the two thirds: the cube root of

325
00:11:55,149 --> 00:11:58,282
eight is two, and two squared is four.

326
00:11:58,319 --> 00:12:00,017
And now Marco's question.

327
00:12:00,055 --> 00:12:02,546
Two hours is half of one doubling.

328
00:12:02,621 --> 00:12:04,885
Say two hours multiplies the height by some

329
00:12:05,074 --> 00:12:05,980
number r.

330
00:12:06,017 --> 00:12:08,508
Then four hours multiplies it by r times

331
00:12:08,659 --> 00:12:10,697
r, and that has to be two.

332
00:12:10,772 --> 00:12:13,149
So r is the square root of two,

333
00:12:13,187 --> 00:12:14,734
two to the one half.

334
00:12:14,772 --> 00:12:16,697
About one point four one four.

335
00:12:16,734 --> 00:12:18,697
Halfway would be six centimeters.

336
00:12:19,149 --> 00:12:22,244
But four times one point four one four...

337
00:12:22,319 --> 00:12:24,055
is about five point seven.

338
00:12:24,558 --> 00:12:25,414
He checks the jar.

339
00:12:25,866 --> 00:12:27,036
Just under six!

340
00:12:27,225 --> 00:12:28,885
Close to what his rule predicts.

341
00:12:29,112 --> 00:12:30,546
Halfway in time...

342
00:12:30,659 --> 00:12:32,206
is not halfway in height.

343
00:12:32,734 --> 00:12:34,357
So where do people trip?

344
00:12:34,621 --> 00:12:35,421
Right here.

345
00:12:35,678 --> 00:12:37,716
Exponent rules work on products,

346
00:12:37,753 --> 00:12:40,244
but they do not work across a plus sign.

347
00:12:40,508 --> 00:12:43,904
x plus y, squared, is not x squared plus

348
00:12:44,055 --> 00:12:44,885
y squared.

349
00:12:45,112 --> 00:12:45,912
Don't believe it?

350
00:12:46,093 --> 00:12:48,734
Test it: one plus two, squared,

351
00:12:48,810 --> 00:12:50,319
is nine, not five.

352
00:12:50,772 --> 00:12:52,508
The square root of the whole thing,

353
00:12:52,659 --> 00:12:55,904
x squared plus nine, is not x plus three.

354
00:12:56,131 --> 00:12:58,772
Try x equals four: the square root of twenty

355
00:12:58,848 --> 00:13:01,112
five is five, not seven.

356
00:13:01,338 --> 00:13:03,602
Testing with a number takes ten seconds,

357
00:13:03,678 --> 00:13:05,678
and it catches most of these slips.

358
00:13:05,942 --> 00:13:06,742
Your turn.

359
00:13:06,848 --> 00:13:08,659
Write six over x squared,

360
00:13:08,734 --> 00:13:10,319
plus the square root of x,

361
00:13:10,357 --> 00:13:12,206
as a sum of powers of x.

362
00:13:12,433 --> 00:13:15,149
Then evaluate twenty seven to the two thirds.

363
00:13:22,078 --> 00:13:24,946
Six over x squared is six x to the negative

364
00:13:25,021 --> 00:13:25,680
two.

365
00:13:25,700 --> 00:13:27,210
The six stays on top.

366
00:13:27,587 --> 00:13:30,455
The square root of x is x to the one half.

367
00:13:30,682 --> 00:13:32,644
For twenty seven to the two thirds,

368
00:13:32,757 --> 00:13:35,210
take the root first: the cube root of twenty

369
00:13:35,285 --> 00:13:38,380
seven is three, and three squared is nine.

370
00:13:38,669 --> 00:13:41,248
The traps: putting the six under the power

371
00:13:41,399 --> 00:13:45,172
too, which means one over thirty six x squared.

372
00:13:45,248 --> 00:13:47,889
Or multiplying twenty seven by two thirds

373
00:13:47,965 --> 00:13:49,097
to get eighteen.

374
00:13:52,597 --> 00:13:53,729
Let's put it all together.

375
00:13:54,182 --> 00:13:56,823
A function gives one output per input.

376
00:13:57,200 --> 00:13:59,163
Parentheses around every input,

377
00:13:59,238 --> 00:14:00,370
and watch the domain.

378
00:14:00,748 --> 00:14:03,012
On a graph, f of a is a height.

379
00:14:03,389 --> 00:14:06,031
Filled dots count, open circles don't,

380
00:14:06,106 --> 00:14:08,370
and piecewise rules switch at the boundary.

381
00:14:08,748 --> 00:14:10,559
Composites work inside out,

382
00:14:10,634 --> 00:14:12,974
and f of x minus h moves right.

383
00:14:13,351 --> 00:14:14,333
And exponents?

384
00:14:14,710 --> 00:14:16,295
Negative means one over.

385
00:14:16,597 --> 00:14:17,955
A fraction means a root.

386
00:14:18,257 --> 00:14:20,370
And nothing crosses a plus sign.

387
00:14:23,870 --> 00:14:24,700
One in the morning.

388
00:14:24,776 --> 00:14:27,417
The starter touches the rubber band: eight

389
00:14:27,568 --> 00:14:29,304
centimeters, right on time!

390
00:14:29,757 --> 00:14:31,342
So Marco mixes the dough.

391
00:14:31,870 --> 00:14:34,059
By morning, the loaves are out of the oven,

392
00:14:34,097 --> 00:14:36,097
and the sign on the door turns to open.

393
00:14:36,625 --> 00:14:38,210
You'll find the notes and practice for this

394
00:14:38,285 --> 00:14:40,965
topic, free, on levlprep.com.

395
00:14:41,229 --> 00:14:42,285
And next time?

396
00:14:42,512 --> 00:14:44,625
The starter was growing at every single

397
00:14:44,700 --> 00:14:45,500
instant.

398
00:14:45,863 --> 00:14:48,248
But how fast was it growing at exactly eleven

399
00:14:48,323 --> 00:14:49,380
o'clock?

400
00:14:49,493 --> 00:14:51,040
That's the question limits were made to

401
00:14:51,116 --> 00:14:51,916
answer.

